1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,391
Hello again, hackers. Today, we are not going to smash the stack with a long string. We are

2
00:00:07,391 --> 00:00:14,783
going to do something quieter. We are going to walk into a shop, buy something, and somehow make

3
00:00:14,783 --> 00:00:22,174
the shop pay us. Not with a race condition, not with a leaked address, not with shellcode. Just

4
00:00:22,174 --> 00:00:26,280
with a number that became too large to remain itself.

5
00:00:26,280 --> 00:00:34,229
Before we zoom in on the bug, we need the crime scene. A real challenge gives us artifacts:

6
00:00:34,229 --> 00:00:42,178
source code, a compiled program, command output, maybe disassembly, maybe debugger state. The rule is simple: no floating

7
00:00:42,178 --> 00:00:50,128
explanation detached from evidence. Every close-up we use today comes from somewhere: a file, a line number, a

8
00:00:50,128 --> 00:00:58,077
command, an address, or a selected row in the disassembler. So first, let's lay the whole program on

9
00:00:58,077 --> 00:00:58,960
the table.

10
00:00:58,960 --> 00:01:06,550
The program has two products. The real flag costs one hundred thousand dollars. We only have eleven hundred,

11
00:01:06,550 --> 00:01:14,140
so that path is locked. But there is a cheaper product: the knockoff flag. The user enters a

12
00:01:14,140 --> 00:01:21,730
quantity. The program calculates a total cost. Then it checks: is the total cost less than or equal

13
00:01:21,730 --> 00:01:29,320
to our balance? If yes, subtract the cost from our balance. That sounds reasonable. But the bug is

14
00:01:29,320 --> 00:01:36,910
hiding in the phrase total cost. The program does not calculate with mathematical integers. It calculates with C

15
00:01:36,910 --> 00:01:39,440
integers. And C integers have walls.

16
00:01:39,440 --> 00:01:47,344
A beginner sees this and thinks: if I buy more flags, the cost gets bigger. One flag: nine

17
00:01:47,344 --> 00:01:55,248
hundred. Two flags: eighteen hundred. Three flags: twenty-seven hundred. Eventually the price is too high, and the shop

18
00:01:55,248 --> 00:02:03,152
says no. That is true in school arithmetic. But a computer integer is not an endless number line.

19
00:02:03,152 --> 00:02:11,056
It is more like an odometer. When every digit is full, the next increment does not create a

20
00:02:11,056 --> 00:02:18,960
new digit. It rolls over. And in signed arithmetic, the other side of that rollover can look negative.

21
00:02:18,960 --> 00:02:27,066
On a common thirty-two-bit signed integer, the values usually run from negative two billion one hundred forty-seven million

22
00:02:27,066 --> 00:02:35,172
four hundred eighty-three thousand six hundred forty-eight, up to positive two billion one hundred forty-seven million four hundred

23
00:02:35,172 --> 00:02:43,279
eighty-three thousand six hundred forty-seven. That top value is INT_MAX. As bits, it is zero followed by thirty-one

24
00:02:43,279 --> 00:02:51,385
ones. The zero at the front means positive. If arithmetic pushes the bit pattern past that edge, the

25
00:02:51,385 --> 00:02:59,491
top bit becomes one. And in two's complement, that same bit pattern is now read as negative. In

26
00:02:59,491 --> 00:03:07,597
C, signed overflow itself is undefined behavior. For this challenge, we explain the observed compiled behavior as two's-complement

27
00:03:07,597 --> 00:03:11,200
wrapping, then later show how sanitizers catch it.

28
00:03:11,200 --> 00:03:19,106
Now return to the shop. The vulnerable calculation is nine hundred times quantity. We choose a quantity that

29
00:03:19,106 --> 00:03:27,012
is positive, because it has to pass the check number flags greater than zero. But we choose it

30
00:03:27,012 --> 00:03:34,918
so large that nine hundred times quantity does not fit inside a signed thirty-two-bit integer. For example, three

31
00:03:34,918 --> 00:03:42,825
million fake flags. Mathematically, that should cost two billion seven hundred million. But inside this thirty-two-bit result, the

32
00:03:42,825 --> 00:03:50,731
lower bits are kept, the sign bit is set, and the result is interpreted as negative one billion

33
00:03:50,731 --> 00:03:58,637
five hundred ninety-four million nine hundred sixty-seven thousand two hundred ninety-six. The shop thinks the final cost is

34
00:03:58,637 --> 00:04:06,543
negative. Then the next line becomes beautiful and terrible: balance equals balance minus total cost. Subtracting a negative

35
00:04:06,543 --> 00:04:08,300
cost is adding money.

36
00:04:08,300 --> 00:04:16,897
So the exploit is not: type a big number because big numbers are funny. The exploit is a

37
00:04:16,897 --> 00:04:25,494
chain. One: the input is accepted because it is positive. Two: multiplication overflows. Three: the overflowed cost becomes

38
00:04:25,494 --> 00:04:34,091
negative. Four: the bounds check asks the wrong question. Five: the balance update subtracts the negative value. Six:

39
00:04:34,091 --> 00:04:42,689
the real flag check sees a balance greater than one hundred thousand. The program is not confused at

40
00:04:42,689 --> 00:04:48,420
the end. It is perfectly obeying the bad arithmetic from the beginning.

41
00:04:48,420 --> 00:04:55,620
This is an integer vulnerability. Not because integers are bad. Because the program used an integer result as

42
00:04:55,620 --> 00:05:02,820
if it were trustworthy proof about reality. The source wanted to prove that the user can afford this

43
00:05:02,820 --> 00:05:10,020
purchase. But the program only proved that the wrapped int value is less than the balance. Those are

44
00:05:10,020 --> 00:05:17,220
not the same statement. Most integer bugs are exactly this kind of mismatch: the programmer checks one world,

45
00:05:17,220 --> 00:05:19,620
but the CPU computes in another.

46
00:05:19,620 --> 00:05:26,830
Now we widen the lens. Signedness bugs happen when the same bits are read with different rules. Take

47
00:05:26,830 --> 00:05:34,040
zero x f f f f f f f f. As unsigned, it is four billion two hundred

48
00:05:34,040 --> 00:05:41,250
ninety-four million nine hundred sixty-seven thousand two hundred ninety-five. As signed thirty-two-bit two's complement, it is negative one.

49
00:05:41,250 --> 00:05:48,459
Same bits. Different story. That is why signedness bugs feel haunted. The value did not change. The reader

50
00:05:48,459 --> 00:05:48,860
changed.

51
00:05:48,860 --> 00:05:57,197
Multiplication is not special. Addition can do the same thing. picoCTF has a tiny challenge called two-sum. The

52
00:05:57,197 --> 00:06:05,534
program adds two signed integers. If two positive numbers produce a negative result, the program knows the addition

53
00:06:05,534 --> 00:06:13,871
wrapped. So the classic input is two billion one hundred forty-seven million four hundred eighty-three thousand six hundred

54
00:06:13,871 --> 00:06:22,207
forty-seven plus one. In school arithmetic, that is two billion one hundred forty-seven million four hundred eighty-three thousand

55
00:06:22,207 --> 00:06:30,544
six hundred forty-eight. In a thirty-two-bit signed interpretation, the wheel crosses the edge and lands on negative two

56
00:06:30,544 --> 00:06:38,881
billion one hundred forty-seven million four hundred eighty-three thousand six hundred forty-eight. The sum did not become logically

57
00:06:38,881 --> 00:06:41,660
smaller. The container was too small.

58
00:06:41,660 --> 00:06:49,262
Overflow is crossing the ceiling. Underflow is crossing the floor. If an unsigned counter is zero, and you

59
00:06:49,262 --> 00:06:56,864
subtract one, it does not become negative one. Unsigned integers do not have negative values. So the wheel

60
00:06:56,864 --> 00:07:04,466
rolls backward from zero to the maximum value. Zero minus one becomes UINT_MAX. That can turn a tiny

61
00:07:04,466 --> 00:07:12,068
mistake into a huge length, a loop that never ends, or an index that points nowhere near where

62
00:07:12,068 --> 00:07:14,180
it was supposed to point.

63
00:07:14,180 --> 00:07:21,784
Another integer bug is truncation. That means a large value is moved into a smaller container. Imagine a

64
00:07:21,784 --> 00:07:29,388
thirty-two-bit integer being stored into a sixteen-bit short. The high bits do not fit, so they are cut

65
00:07:29,388 --> 00:07:36,992
away. The program may think it validated a large number, then later the shortened value means something completely

66
00:07:36,992 --> 00:07:44,597
different. This is especially dangerous when the truncated value becomes negative, passes a bad check, and then gets

67
00:07:44,597 --> 00:07:52,201
converted back into a huge unsigned size. CWE eight three nine gives this family of mistake: a value

68
00:07:52,201 --> 00:07:58,960
can become a negative short, pass a maximum-only check, then become a huge unsigned copy size.

69
00:07:58,960 --> 00:08:05,421
A bounds check needs two walls: a maximum wall and a minimum wall. Many vulnerable checks only ask:

70
00:08:05,421 --> 00:08:11,882
is this value too big? But they forget to ask: is this value negative? That is how negative

71
00:08:11,882 --> 00:08:18,343
indexes happen. That is how negative lengths happen. That is how a value can look safe to the

72
00:08:18,343 --> 00:08:24,803
check, then become dangerous to the function that receives it. The right shape is not just x less

73
00:08:24,803 --> 00:08:31,264
than or equal to max. It is zero less than or equal to x, and x less than

74
00:08:31,264 --> 00:08:32,700
or equal to max.

75
00:08:32,700 --> 00:08:40,551
Now we reach one of the most common dangerous shapes. A signed length enters the program. Len equals

76
00:08:40,551 --> 00:08:48,402
negative one. The program checks: if len is less than or equal to five hundred twelve. That passes,

77
00:08:48,402 --> 00:08:56,253
because negative one is definitely less than five hundred twelve. But then the program calls something like memcpy.

78
00:08:56,253 --> 00:09:04,104
And memcpy does not take an int length. It takes a size_t: an unsigned size. So negative one

79
00:09:04,104 --> 00:09:11,955
becomes a huge positive number. Suddenly a check that looked safe becomes a request to copy nearly everything.

80
00:09:11,955 --> 00:09:15,880
CWE one nine five describes this signed-to-unsigned conversion failure.

81
00:09:15,880 --> 00:09:24,622
Now take the flag shop formula: price times quantity. Replace price with element size. Replace quantity with element

82
00:09:24,622 --> 00:09:33,363
count. Now it says sizeof element times count. That result is used to allocate memory. If the multiplication

83
00:09:33,363 --> 00:09:42,105
overflows, the program allocates a small buffer. But the rest of the program still believes the original large

84
00:09:42,105 --> 00:09:50,847
count. So allocation uses the wrapped number, while the write loop uses the real number. That gap is

85
00:09:50,847 --> 00:09:59,588
the vulnerability. This is the heap version of the shop bug. In the shop, overflow created fake money.

86
00:09:59,588 --> 00:10:08,330
On the heap, overflow creates fake space. CERT's INT thirty C examples warn that unsigned multiplication wrap in

87
00:10:08,330 --> 00:10:17,072
allocation-size calculations can allocate insufficient memory. CWE one nine zero notes that too little memory can make integer

88
00:10:17,072 --> 00:10:19,500
overflow primary to buffer overflow.

89
00:10:19,500 --> 00:10:28,430
This is why integer bugs matter in exploitation. They often begin as logic bugs: no crash, no obvious

90
00:10:28,430 --> 00:10:37,360
overwrite, just a wrong number. But then that wrong number becomes a memory boundary. Once the memory boundary

91
00:10:37,360 --> 00:10:46,290
is wrong, a normal write becomes an out-of-bounds write. Integer overflow into undersized allocation. Undersized allocation into heap

92
00:10:46,290 --> 00:10:55,219
overflow. Heap overflow into corrupted metadata, overwritten pointers, or changed program state. The first bug is arithmetic. The

93
00:10:55,219 --> 00:10:57,700
final consequence is memory corruption.

94
00:10:57,700 --> 00:11:05,078
Integer overflow can also happen before an array access. Imagine the program checks index plus length less than

95
00:11:05,078 --> 00:11:12,455
or equal to buffer size. That sounds fine. But if index plus length wraps around, the sum becomes

96
00:11:12,455 --> 00:11:19,833
small again. The check passes. Then the program uses the original index and length to read or write.

97
00:11:19,833 --> 00:11:27,210
This is index wraparound. The value took a secret lap around the integer wheel before the guard saw

98
00:11:27,210 --> 00:11:27,620
it.

99
00:11:27,620 --> 00:11:35,450
The same idea exists with pointers. A pointer is an address. An offset is a number added to

100
00:11:35,450 --> 00:11:43,280
that address. If the offset calculation overflows, or if base plus length crosses the address boundary, the resulting

101
00:11:43,280 --> 00:11:51,110
pointer may appear to be inside a safe region, even though the intended range was outside it. This

102
00:11:51,110 --> 00:11:58,940
is why integer values used in pointer arithmetic are security-sensitive. An offset is not just a number. It

103
00:11:58,940 --> 00:12:06,770
is a future address. Check the offset before addition. Check base less than or equal to end. Check

104
00:12:06,770 --> 00:12:11,120
length less than or equal to object size minus offset.

105
00:12:11,120 --> 00:12:19,279
One more trap: not every integer type has the same width on every architecture. On many sixty-four-bit systems,

106
00:12:19,279 --> 00:12:27,438
pointers and size_t are sixty-four bits, but int often remains thirty-two bits. On LP64 systems, long and pointers

107
00:12:27,438 --> 00:12:35,596
are sixty-four bits. On LLP64 systems, used by Windows, long stays thirty-two bits while pointers are sixty-four bits.

108
00:12:35,596 --> 00:12:43,755
So code that seemed safe on one target can become suspicious on another. A cast from size_t down

109
00:12:43,755 --> 00:12:51,914
to int, a length stored in short, a pointer difference stored in the wrong type: these are places

110
00:12:51,914 --> 00:12:55,540
where the program may silently throw away bits.

111
00:12:55,540 --> 00:13:03,500
Source code says total cost equals nine hundred times number flags. But the CPU does not know flags.

112
00:13:03,500 --> 00:13:11,461
It knows registers. It knows multiplication. It knows that a register has a fixed width. So in this

113
00:13:11,461 --> 00:13:19,421
local reconstruction, we compile the supplied source and open the disassembly. The multiply instruction is at address zero

114
00:13:19,421 --> 00:13:27,381
x four zero zero six two d. It is imul eax, eax, zero x three eight four. The

115
00:13:27,381 --> 00:13:35,341
immediate zero x three eight four is nine hundred. EAX holds the low thirty-two-bit result, then the program

116
00:13:35,341 --> 00:13:43,302
stores it as total cost. That is the bridge from C to silicon. We also attempted the optional

117
00:13:43,302 --> 00:13:51,262
UBSan build. The host linker is missing its libubsan runtime, so there is no sanitizer transcript here. We

118
00:13:51,262 --> 00:13:54,800
show that limitation instead of making one up.

119
00:13:54,800 --> 00:14:02,991
The fix is not: use a bigger integer and pray. Bigger containers only move the wall farther away.

120
00:14:02,991 --> 00:14:11,182
The fix is validate before the operation that can overflow. For flag shop, ask: is the quantity positive?

121
00:14:11,182 --> 00:14:19,373
Is the quantity small enough that nine hundred times quantity fits? Then calculate. Then compare. Or use checked

122
00:14:19,373 --> 00:14:27,564
arithmetic. In modern C, C23 adds checked integer helpers. Many compilers also have overflow builtins. The important idea

123
00:14:27,564 --> 00:14:35,755
is the same: make overflow a branch, not a surprise. The input range gate comes before multiplication. The

124
00:14:35,755 --> 00:14:38,940
affordability gate comes after a successful calculation.

125
00:14:38,940 --> 00:14:47,322
When you audit C or C++ code, look for integers that become decisions. Not every integer matters equally.

126
00:14:47,322 --> 00:14:55,705
Dangerous ones are sizes, lengths, counts, indexes, offsets, allocation sizes, loop bounds, pointer arithmetic, and security checks. If

127
00:14:55,705 --> 00:15:04,087
user input touches one of these values, trace it. What type is it? How wide is it? Is

128
00:15:04,087 --> 00:15:12,469
it signed? Does it get cast? Is it added? Is it multiplied? Is it checked before or after

129
00:15:12,469 --> 00:15:20,852
the operation? Most importantly: is the checked value the same value that is later used? If allocation checks

130
00:15:20,852 --> 00:15:26,440
one number and copying uses another, the exploit lives in the gap.

131
00:15:26,440 --> 00:15:34,984
Integer vulnerabilities are strange because they often begin without memory corruption. The program just believes a number. But

132
00:15:34,984 --> 00:15:43,528
in low-level code, numbers become boundaries. Boundaries become buffers. Buffers become control. In flag shop, the number made

133
00:15:43,528 --> 00:15:52,071
us rich. In real programs, the same pattern can make a buffer too small, a copy too large,

134
00:15:52,071 --> 00:16:00,615
an index negative, or a pointer wrap around the edge of memory. So when you see arithmetic near

135
00:16:00,615 --> 00:16:09,159
memory, slow down. Ask what world the program is checking: the mathematical world, or the machine world? Because

136
00:16:09,159 --> 00:16:14,380
the exploit usually begins in the difference. Until next time, hackers.
